eme Metal", "context": "European metal festivals, such as Wacken Open Air, have featured extreme metal bands since the early 2000s."}], "places": [{"name": "Birmingham, England", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Major Scene"}, {"name": "Stockholm, Sweden", "role": "Metal Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: extreme metal", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "extreme-metal"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q465978", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extreme_metal"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "extreme metal contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Extreme metal significantly shaped modern music by influencing countless artists and genres, exemplified by the emergence of subgenres like metalcore, which have reached mainstream audiences.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:58:37Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Any of a number of related heavy metal music subgenres Created c. 1977.", "newValue": "Extreme metal is a collection of heavy metal music subgenres that emerged around 1977, characterized by its aggressive sound, complex structures, and often dark themes. This genre includes notable styles such as thrash metal, death metal, black metal, and grindcore, which collectively contributed to the broadening of the heavy metal genre. The origins of extreme metal can be traced back to pioneering bands like Black Sabbath, Motörhead, and Venom, who laid the groundwork for a sound that would evolve into various subgenres, each with distinct characteristics and cultural significance.

By the early 1980s, extreme metal saw the formation of influential bands like Slayer, Metallica, and Iron Maiden, which helped to define the genre's sound and aesthetic. The release of seminal albums such as Metallica's "Kill 'Em All" (1983) and Slayer's "Reign in Blood" (1986) marked significant milestones in the genre's development, garnering critical acclaim and establishing a devoted fan base. As extreme metal gained traction, it became associated with a countercultural movement, providing a voice for disaffected youth and shaping the cultural landscape of the late 20th century.

